---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Experiment 10.4: Radio Waves">
    <main class="container mx-auto px-4 py-8">
        <div class="experiment-header">
            <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-center text-blue-700 mb-2">EXPERIMENT 10.4</h1>
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-center text-blue-700 mb-8">RADIO WAVES</h2>
        </div>

        <!-- Student Information Section -->
        <section class="mb-8 p-6 bg-gray-50 rounded-lg shadow-md">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700 mb-4">STUDENT INFORMATION</h3>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
                <div>
                    <label for="studentName" class="block text-sm font-medium text-gray-700 mb-2">Student Name:</label>
                    <input type="text" id="studentName" class="w-full p-2 border border-gray-300 rounded focus:ring-2 focus:ring-blue-500 focus:border-blue-500" placeholder="Enter your full name" required>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <label for="studentEmail" class="block text-sm font-medium text-gray-700 mb-2">Student Email:</label>
                    <input type="email" id="studentEmail" class="w-full p-2 border border-gray-300 rounded focus:ring-2 focus:ring-blue-500 focus:border-blue-500" placeholder="Enter your email address" required>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700">PURPOSE:</h3>
            <p class="mb-4">To investigate the properties of radio waves and understand how they are used for communication.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700">BACKGROUND:</h3>
            <p class="mb-4">
                Radio waves are a type of electromagnetic radiation with wavelengths longer than infrared light. 
                They have frequencies ranging from about 30 Hz to 300 GHz and are used extensively for various 
                forms of communication, including radio broadcasting, television, mobile phones, and wireless networks.
            </p>
            <p class="mb-4">
                Radio waves were first predicted by James Clerk Maxwell in the 1860s and later demonstrated experimentally 
                by Heinrich Hertz in 1887. Unlike visible light, radio waves can penetrate many materials and travel long 
                distances, making them ideal for communication purposes. They are generated by accelerating electric charges 
                in antennas and can be detected by receivers that convert them back into sound, images, or data.
            </p>
            <p class="mb-4">
                In this experiment, we will explore how radio waves are transmitted and received, and how different 
                materials affect their propagation.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700">MATERIALS:</h3>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-8 mb-4">
                <li>AM/FM radio receiver (portable radio)</li>
                <li>Aluminum foil</li>
                <li>Metal mesh screen or wire mesh (like a window screen)</li>
                <li>Plastic wrap</li>
                <li>Glass container</li>
                <li>Water</li>
                <li>Salt</li>
                <li>Metal pot or pan with lid</li>
                <li>Cardboard box</li>
                <li>Ruler or measuring tape</li>
                <li>Optional: Two-way radios (walkie-talkies)</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700">SAFETY PRECAUTIONS:</h3>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-8 mb-4">
                <li>This experiment involves no significant safety hazards</li>
                <li>Be careful when handling glass containers</li>
                <li>Keep electrical devices away from water</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700">PROCEDURE:</h3>
            <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2">Part 1: Radio Wave Shielding</h4>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-8 mb-4">
                <li class="mb-2">
                    Turn on the radio and tune it to a clear station (AM or FM).
                </li>
                <li class="mb-2">
                    Adjust the volume to a comfortable level where you can clearly hear the broadcast.
                </li>
                <li class="mb-2">
                    Place the radio in the center of a table and note the quality of reception.
                </li>
                <li class="mb-2">
                    Cover the radio with different materials one at a time and observe any changes in reception:
                    <ul class="list-disc pl-8 mb-2">
                        <li>Aluminum foil</li>
                        <li>Metal mesh screen</li>
                        <li>Plastic wrap</li>
                        <li>Glass container</li>
                        <li>Cardboard box</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li class="mb-2">
                    For each material, rate the signal quality on a scale from 1 (no reception) to 5 (perfect reception).
                </li>
                <li class="mb-2">
                    Place the radio inside a metal pot or pan and close the lid. Observe what happens to the reception.
                </li>
            </ol>

            <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2">Part 2: Effect of Distance on Radio Waves</h4>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-8 mb-4">
                <li class="mb-2">
                    If you have two-way radios (walkie-talkies), turn them on and set them to the same channel.
                </li>
                <li class="mb-2">
                    Start with the radios next to each other and test that they are working properly.
                </li>
                <li class="mb-2">
                    Have a partner take one radio and walk away in increments of 10 meters (or other convenient distance).
                </li>
                <li class="mb-2">
                    At each distance, test the communication quality and record your observations.
                </li>
                <li class="mb-2">
                    Continue until you reach a distance where communication becomes difficult or impossible.
                </li>
                <li class="mb-2">
                    Repeat the test with obstacles (like walls or trees) between the radios and note any differences.
                </li>
            </ol>

            <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2">Part 3: Effect of Water and Salt on Radio Waves</h4>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-8 mb-4">
                <li class="mb-2">
                    Fill the glass container with water.
                </li>
                <li class="mb-2">
                    Turn on the radio and tune it to a clear station.
                </li>
                <li class="mb-2">
                    Place the radio near the container of water and observe any changes in reception.
                </li>
                <li class="mb-2">
                    Add salt to the water, stirring until dissolved, and observe if there are any further changes in reception.
                </li>
                <li class="mb-2">
                    Try placing the radio at different positions around the container and note any variations in signal quality.
                </li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <!-- Analysis Questions Section -->
        <section class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700 mb-4">ANALYSIS QUESTIONS:</h3>
            
            <div class="mb-4">
                <p class="font-semibold mb-2">1. Which materials were most effective at blocking radio waves? Why do you think this is the case?</p>
                <textarea id="analysis1" rows="3" class="w-full p-2 border border-gray-300 rounded"></textarea>
            </div>
            
            <div class="mb-4">
                <p class="font-semibold mb-2">2. How did distance affect the quality of radio wave transmission? Was the relationship linear or did it follow another pattern?</p>
                <textarea id="analysis2" rows="3" class="w-full p-2 border border-gray-300 rounded"></textarea>
            </div>
            
            <div class="mb-4">
                <p class="font-semibold mb-2">3. How did obstacles affect radio wave transmission compared to just distance alone?</p>
                <textarea id="analysis3" rows="3" class="w-full p-2 border border-gray-300 rounded"></textarea>
            </div>
            
            <div class="mb-4">
                <p class="font-semibold mb-2">4. What effect did water have on radio wave transmission? How did adding salt change this effect?</p>
                <textarea id="analysis4" rows="3" class="w-full p-2 border border-gray-300 rounded"></textarea>
            </div>
            
            <div class="mb-4">
                <p class="font-semibold mb-2">5. Based on your observations, explain why radio waves are useful for communication over long distances and through certain obstacles.</p>
                <textarea id="analysis5" rows="3" class="w-full p-2 border border-gray-300 rounded"></textarea>
            </div>
            
            <div class="mb-4">
                <p class="font-semibold mb-2">6. How might the properties of radio waves that you observed in this experiment be used in everyday technology?</p>
                <textarea id="analysis6" rows="3" class="w-full p-2 border border-gray-300 rounded"></textarea>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Conclusion Section -->
        <section class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700 mb-4">CONCLUSION:</h3>
            <p class="mb-4">Summarize your findings about radio waves and their properties. Discuss how different materials affect radio wave transmission and how this relates to real-world applications. Include what you learned about the effect of distance and obstacles on radio wave communication.</p>
            <textarea id="conclusion" rows="6" class="w-full p-2 border border-gray-300 rounded"></textarea>
        </section>

        <!-- Email Submission Section -->
        <section id="submission-section" class="mb-8 p-6 bg-blue-50 rounded-lg shadow-md">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700 mb-4">SUBMIT YOUR RESULTS</h3>
            <p class="mb-4">Click the button below to submit your experiment results to your teacher.</p>
            <button id="submitResults" class="bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white font-bold py-2 px-4 rounded">
                Submit to Teacher
            </button>
            <div id="submissionMessage" class="mt-4 hidden"></div>
        </section>
    </main>
</Layout>

<script>
    // Initialize EmailJS
    (function() {
        emailjs.init("ct_s19oZHppLy9BlW");
    })();

    // Get form elements - only access existing elements
    const submitButton = document.getElementById('submitResults');
    const submissionMessage = document.getElementById('submissionMessage');

    // Function to show submission message
    function showSubmissionMessage(message, isError = false) {
        if (submissionMessage) {
            submissionMessage.textContent = message;
            submissionMessage.classList.remove('hidden', 'text-green-600', 'text-red-600');
            submissionMessage.classList.add(isError ? 'text-red-600' : 'text-green-600');
        }
    }

    // Handle form submission - only access existing elements
    if (submitButton) {
        submitButton.addEventListener('click', function() {
            // Collect analysis answers from existing elements
            const analysis1 = document.getElementById('analysis1')?.value || '';
            const analysis2 = document.getElementById('analysis2')?.value || '';
            const analysis3 = document.getElementById('analysis3')?.value || '';
            const analysis4 = document.getElementById('analysis4')?.value || '';
            const analysis5 = document.getElementById('analysis5')?.value || '';
            const analysis6 = document.getElementById('analysis6')?.value || '';
            const conclusion = document.getElementById('conclusion')?.value || '';
            
            // Get student information from input fields
            const studentName = document.getElementById('studentName')?.value?.trim() || 'Student';
            const studentEmail = document.getElementById('studentEmail')?.value?.trim() || 'chuck@c00lnerd.com';
            const today = new Date().toISOString().split('T')[0];
            
            // Create comprehensive experiment data with all analysis questions
            const conclusionText = `
EXPERIMENT 10.4: RADIO WAVES RESULTS

STUDENT: ${studentName}
DATE: ${today}

ANALYSIS QUESTIONS:
1. Which materials were most effective at blocking radio waves? Why do you think this is the case?
${analysis1 || 'Not answered'}

2. How did distance affect the quality of radio wave transmission? Was the relationship linear or did it follow another pattern?
${analysis2 || 'Not answered'}

3. How did obstacles affect radio wave transmission compared to just distance alone?
${analysis3 || 'Not answered'}

4. What effect did water have on radio wave transmission? How did adding salt change this effect?
${analysis4 || 'Not answered'}

5. Based on your observations, explain why radio waves are useful for communication over long distances and through certain obstacles.
${analysis5 || 'Not answered'}

6. How might the properties of radio waves that you observed in this experiment be used in everyday technology?
${analysis6 || 'Not answered'}

CONCLUSION:
${conclusion || 'No conclusion provided'}

EXPERIMENT SUMMARY:
This experiment investigates radio wave properties including transmission, blocking materials, distance effects, and real-world applications in communication technology.
            `.trim();
            
            // Check for missing elements
            const missingElements = [];
            if (!analysis1) missingElements.push('Analysis Q1');
            if (!analysis2) missingElements.push('Analysis Q2');
            if (!analysis3) missingElements.push('Analysis Q3');
            if (!analysis4) missingElements.push('Analysis Q4');
            if (!analysis5) missingElements.push('Analysis Q5');
            if (!analysis6) missingElements.push('Analysis Q6');
            if (!conclusion) missingElements.push('Conclusion');
            
            const missingElementsText = missingElements.length > 0 
                ? `Missing: ${missingElements.join(', ')} ❌` 
                : 'All sections complete ✅';
            
            // Show loading state
            submitButton.textContent = 'Sending...';
            showSubmissionMessage('Sending your results...');
            
            // Send email using EmailJS with proven parameter mapping
            emailjs.send('service_ot1jg6s', 'template_y556rhr', {
                student_name: studentName,
                student_email: studentEmail,
                experiment_title: 'Experiment 10.4: Radio Waves',
                hypothesis: 'Investigating radio wave properties, transmission, and blocking materials',
                missing_elements: missingElementsText,
                conclusion: conclusionText,
                subject: `Experiment 10.4: Radio Waves - ${studentName}`
            })
            .then(function(response) {
                console.log('SUCCESS!', response.status, response.text);
                showSubmissionMessage('✓ Experiment results submitted successfully!');
                submitButton.textContent = 'Submit to Teacher';
            }, function(error) {
                console.log('FAILED...', error);
                showSubmissionMessage('✗ Failed to submit results. Please try again.', true);
                submitButton.textContent = 'Submit to Teacher';
            });
        });
    }
</script>
